//? Title: Euclidean Distance Transform: Actual Parabola Envelopes and Nearest Sites
//? Subtitle: A hand-checkable triangle, the original three-site field, safe empty shapes and measured linear work
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a rectangular grid with marked sites, we want each cell’s distance
to its nearest site and the coordinates of a site attaining that distance.
Checking every cell against every site scales poorly as both sets grow.
A separable Euclidean distance transform solves the problem through
one-dimensional lower envelopes of parabolas. We start with a 3–4–5
triangle, expose an actual discarded parabola and retain the original
9×15 three-site distance field and middle-row profile. The canonical core
now handles true infinity, empty rows, rectangular validation and long
indices without a finite placeholder or signed integer squares. Exhaustive
small-input checks, nearest-site witnesses, explicit tie rules and measured
sweep counts distinguish the exact geometric metric from floating-point
rounding, image-boundary conventions and obstacle-constrained path lengths.
)

\toc

> Begin with a 3–4–5 triangle between cell centres

Mark site (row 0,column 0) in a 4×5 grid. At query (3,4), the squared
Euclidean distance is 3²+4²=25, and the distance is five grid units.
The selected original site is (0,0). No padding or unmarked outside cell
contributes another candidate. A route restricted to horizontal and vertical
unit moves would instead cost seven; that is a different distance model.

The returned feature field retains both squared values and nearest-site
coordinates. The native diagram uses equal coordinate scales to illustrate
the geometry; every printed value comes from the core transform.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/edt_triangle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const auto grid=dm::geometry::distance_site_grid(4,5,{{0,0}});
  const auto f=dm::geometry::euclidean_distance_transform_features(grid);
  std::print("Query (3,4): squared {:.0f}; distance {:.0f}; nearest ({},{}).\n\n",f.squared_distance[3][4],f.distance[3][4],f.nearest_row[3][4],f.nearest_column[3][4]);
  std::print("| row | squared c0 | c1 | c2 | c3 | c4 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t r=0;r<4;++r)std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |\n",r,f.squared_distance[r][0],f.squared_distance[r][1],f.squared_distance[r][2],f.squared_distance[r][3],f.squared_distance[r][4]);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(95,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke legs;legs.dashed=true;legs.color={148,163,184};legs.width=2;
  d.path({{0,0},{4,0},{4,-3}},legs);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle dot;dot.circle=true;dot.width=.6;dot.height=.6;dot.font_size=22;
  d.node("site",{0,0},"S",dot).node("query",{4,-3},"Q",dot);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;blue.color={37,99,235};blue.width=3;
  d.edge("site","query",std::format("distance {:.0f}",f.distance[3][4]),blue)
   .text({2,.55},"4 columns",22).text({5,-1.5},"3 rows",22)
   .text({2,1.1},"Unit-grid cell centres: 3² + 4² = 25",25)
   .text({2,-3.85},"Site S = (row 0, column 0); query Q = (row 3, column 4)",20)
   .save_svg("build/edt_triangle.svg");
}
```
