// result_end
\caption(Coloured curves show the three candidate quadratics; black markers are the five computed sampled minima. The red candidate is actually popped from the envelope, and the tie at query two keeps index zero.)
\alttext(Three parabolas are overlaid with black minimum markers at integer queries zero through four; the candidate rooted at two stays above the minimum.)

> Why the transform is linear and separable

Let K be the number of finite candidates on one line. Every candidate is
pushed once, and any removed candidate is popped once. With K>0, each
candidate after the first performs one comparison that ends its search,
plus one comparison for every earlier candidate it removes. Thus the
actual count is intersections=K−1+pops, bounded by 2K−2.
The winner pointer only advances, so its total moves are at most the final
envelope length minus one. A nonempty envelope evaluates one parabola
per output sample; an all-infinity line has no finite evaluations.
Together with the input scan, this gives O(n) time and storage per line.

For a binary site mask, seed cost zero at each site and infinity elsewhere.
The squared distance at (r,c) separates:

\[
\min_{(a,b)\;\text{site}}\{(r-a)^2+(c-b)^2\}
=\min_b\left\{(c-b)^2+\min_{a:(a,b)\;\text{site}}(r-a)^2\right\}.
\]

First transform each column to obtain its vertical squared costs, then
transform those rows. A final square root gives Euclidean distance.
Nearest-site coordinates follow the same minimizers: the row pass chooses
a column and its stored vertical winner supplies the original row.
An exact two-dimensional tie chooses the smaller column, then the smaller
row. That deterministic convention affects witnesses, not the distance.

For a nonempty shape with R rows, C columns and N=R*C cells, the two passes inspect
2N samples across R+C lines. Finite evaluations are at most 2N.
Envelope comparisons remain linear in those inputs, regardless of site
count. Shape validation and outer row storage add O(R), including zero-width
rows. The ordinary result is an R-by-C distance matrix; optional feature
and column-snapshot arrays also use O(N) storage, with O(R+C) line scratch.
No per-cell list of all sites is scanned by the transform.

[Felzenszwalb and Huttenlocher’s published account](https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2012.v008a019)
describes the generalized quadratic transform and its separable construction.
“Exact Euclidean” identifies the metric and algorithm, rather than an integer
chamfer approximation. Intersections use floating arithmetic, output squared
costs use double and square roots are rounded. Arbitrarily large or poorly
separated weighted costs are not promised arbitrary-precision minimizers.

> Retain the original 9×15 three-site field

The original sites are (2,3), (6,11) and (4,7), in (row,column) order.
All cells have unit spacing. Nonzero input values mark sites; zero values
are ordinary unmarked cells, not automatically exterior background.
This educational grid is the complete input, with no external dataset.

The full distance table below retains the original one-decimal field.
The heatmap uses downward row labels and a finite colour range, with black
points at sites. Display cells are plot rectangles; distances are computed
on the unit grid independently of canvas proportions. The right panel
retains the original profile along row four. It reaches zero at column
seven, while sites in other rows also influence the profile.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/edt_original_field.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto grid=dm::geometry::distance_site_grid(9,15,{{2,3},{6,11},{4,7}});
  const auto t=dm::geometry::euclidean_distance_transform_trace(grid);const auto& f=t.result;
  std::print("Original grid: 9 rows, 15 columns; sites (2,3), (6,11), (4,7).\n\n| row |");
  for(std::size_t c=0;c<15;++c)std::print(" c{} |",c);std::print("\n| ---: |");for(std::size_t c=0;c<15;++c)std::print(" ---: |");std::print("\n");
  for(std::size_t r=0;r<9;++r){std::print("| {} |",r);for(double value:f.distance[r])std::print(" {:.1f} |",value);std::print("\n");}
  std::print("\nRow 4 distances: ");for(double value:f.distance[4])std::print("{:.1f} ",value);std::print("\n");
  std::print("Column pass at query (4,5): {}; final squared cost {:.0f}; witness ({},{}).\n",t.column_squared[4][5],f.squared_distance[4][5],f.nearest_row[4][5],f.nearest_column[4][5]);
  std::vector<double> columns;for(std::size_t c=0;c<15;++c)columns.push_back(c);
  auto field=dm::geometry::plot_distance_field(f);auto profile=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  profile.line(columns,f.distance[4],"row 4 distance",{37,99,235}).points(columns,f.distance[4],"",{37,99,235},5)
   .title("Original middle-row distance profile").x_label("column").y_label("Euclidean distance (grid units)").y_limits(-.2,5.2);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(field).add(profile).size(1100,520).title("Original 9×15 mask; three sites and unit cell spacing").fit_panels().save_svg("build/edt_original_field.svg");
}
