> Boundary shapes, tie witnesses and long indices

An empty grid returns empty. A grid containing R empty rows returns R
empty rows; it never indexes a zero-length envelope. Ragged grids are
rejected before output allocation. If there are no sites, distances remain
positive infinity, nearest rows use sentinel R and nearest columns use
sentinel C. The true-infinity implementation also handles site-free columns
without subtracting infinities. It requires no fixed missing-site constant.

The next tie example places sites at (0,2) and (2,0). Query (1,1) is
sqrt(2) from both. The column-first convention selects (2,0), even though
the other site has the smaller row. This is a specified witness convention,
not a different geometric distance. Without obstacles or a graph of allowed
moves, this field does not compute detours around barriers.

The former header squared signed int indices in its intersection formula.
A compiled legacy probe reports signed overflow on a 50,001-sample input;
a separate zero-width probe fails a vector-index assertion. The new kernel
uses size_t indices and converts before arithmetic. A one-row 100,001-cell
control now reaches distance 100,000 and squared distance 10,000,000,000
at the far end. Both the original function name/include and the former
internal detail::dt_1d spelling forward to the canonical compiled code;
normal callers link datamunge as in every report program here.

The one-dimensional trace has an optional complete-sample cap of one million;
the grid trace and site-grid builder have optional complete-cell caps of
one million. Zero disables only those optional caps; count/container checks
still apply. Plotting has a separate default cap of 4096 polygons. The
native tests check exact limits and one-short limits before output capture.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/edt_boundaries.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto empty=dm::geometry::euclidean_distance_transform_trace({{}, {}});
  const auto absent=dm::geometry::euclidean_distance_transform_features({{0,0},{0,0}});
  const auto negative=dm::geometry::squared_distance_transform_1d({-4,std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity(),0});
  const auto f=dm::geometry::euclidean_distance_transform_features(dm::geometry::distance_site_grid(3,3,{{0,2},{2,0}}));
  std::print("Zero-width shape: {} empty rows, {} sweeps. No-site query: distance {}, row sentinel {}, column sentinel {}.\n",empty.result.distance.size(),empty.work.lines,absent.distance[1][1],absent.nearest_row[1][1],absent.nearest_column[1][1]);
  std::print("Generalized negative costs produce {} without a square root.\n",negative);
  std::print("Tie at (1,1): squared {:.0f}, distance {:.6f}, selected site ({},{}).\n",f.squared_distance[1][1],f.distance[1][1],f.nearest_row[1][1],f.nearest_column[1][1]);
  std::size_t rejected=0;
  try{(void)dm::geometry::euclidean_distance_transform({{1,0},{}});}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::geometry::squared_distance_transform_1d({std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN()});}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::geometry::euclidean_distance_transform_trace({{1,0},{0,0}},3);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::geometry::squared_distance_transform_1d_trace({0,1,2},2);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  if(rejected!=4)return 1;std::print("Expected ragged/cost/grid-cap/line-cap rejections: {}.\n",rejected);
  const auto long_grid=dm::geometry::distance_site_grid(1,100001,{{0,0}});const auto long_field=dm::geometry::euclidean_distance_transform_features(long_grid);
  std::print("Long row: 100001 cells; far distance {:.0f}; squared {:.0f}; witness ({},{}).\n",long_field.distance[0].back(),long_field.squared_distance[0].back(),long_field.nearest_row[0].back(),long_field.nearest_column[0].back());
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(140,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke grid;grid.color={226,232,240};grid.width=1;
  for(double k:{0.,1.,2.}){d.path({{0,-k},{2,-k}},grid).path({{k,0},{k,-2}},grid);}
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle dot;dot.circle=true;dot.width=.5;dot.height=.5;dot.font_size=20;
  d.node("a",{0,-2},"A",dot).node("b",{2,0},"B",dot).node("q",{1,-1},"Q",dot);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;blue.color={37,99,235};blue.width=3;dm::graphics::DiagramStroke other;other.color={148,163,184};other.dashed=true;
  d.edge("q","a","",blue).edge("q","b","",other)
   .text({1,.7},"Equal distances; smaller column wins the witness tie",23)
   .text({1,-2.7},"A = (row 2, col 0); B = (row 0, col 2); Q = (row 1, col 1)",18)
   .save_svg("build/edt_boundaries.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Zero-width shape: 2 empty rows, 0 sweeps. No-site query: distance inf, row sentinel 2, column sentinel 2.
Generalized negative costs produce [-4, -3, 0] without a square root.
Tie at (1,1): squared 2, distance 1.414214, selected site (2,0).
Expected ragged/cost/grid-cap/line-cap rejections: 4.
Long row: 100001 cells; far distance 100000; squared 10000000000; witness (0,0).
\image(figures/edt_boundaries.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Both sites are at squared distance two from the query; the computed witness uses column zero. Printed controls expose empty/no-site shapes, negative generalized costs, validation/cap failures and the long-index result.)
\alttext(A query at the centre of a 3×3 grid has equal-length rays to two corner sites. The selected ray to the smaller-column site is blue; the other is dashed grey.)

> What was verified

Native tests exhaust all 87,381 vectors of lengths zero through eight over
costs {-2,0,3,infinity}, comparing the kernel and legacy helper spelling
against an independent quadratic minimum. They also exhaust all 74,954
binary masks for row and column counts one through four. Direct site
search verifies squared distances, rounded square roots, original-site
witnesses, the explicit column-first tie rule and every column-pass value.
The existing 300 seeded random-grid brute-force checks remain in the suite.

Hand-derived crossings, an actual pop, negative sampled costs, all-infinity
lines, empty/zero-width/ragged shapes, nonzero char sites, cap boundaries,
invalid costs/sites/counts, owned result copies and bounded plot inputs
receive explicit tests. Single-site and all-site work formulas are checked
independently of timings. The 100,001-sample/vector and one-row controls
cross the old signed-square limit without signed products in the kernel.

The original 9×15 field and row profile remain reproducible from short
programs. The new traces explain why the transform is fast and how the
returned nearest-site coordinates are chosen. The result measures direct
Euclidean centre distance on the supplied unit grid; image padding,
anisotropic pixel spacing, geodesic detours and continuous boundary distance
would each define a different problem.

> References

- P. F. Felzenszwalb and D. P. Huttenlocher (2012), “Distance Transforms of
  Sampled Functions”, Theory of Computing 8, 415–428.
  [Published article](https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2012.v008a019),
  [author’s open paper](https://cs.brown.edu/people/pfelzens/papers/dt-final.pdf).
